---
title: Post-interceptors
description: The stage that runs after a successful handler — observing and reshaping the result.
---

A post-interceptor runs after the handler returned successfully, and may replace the result. It
does not run when the handler threw; that is the exception stage.

## The contracts

```csharp
ICommandPostInterceptor                          // every command, untyped result
ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>                // one command, untyped result
ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>       // one command, typed result
```

Queries have the same three. Events have the first two — a broadcast produces no result.

## Observing

```csharp
public sealed class CountSuccesses(IMetrics metrics) : ICommandPostInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        metrics.Increment(command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Return the result you were given. Whatever you return is what the next stage — and eventually
the caller — sees.

## Reshaping

The typed form is the one worth reaching for when you actually touch the result:

```csharp
public sealed class CacheProduct(IMemoryCache cache)
    : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct query, ProductDto result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        cache.Set(query.Id, result, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5));
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Replacing the result is the same shape — return something else:

```csharp
public sealed class Redact : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetCustomer, CustomerDto>
{
    public ValueTask<CustomerDto> HandleAsync(
        GetCustomer query, CustomerDto result, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(result with { Email = "***" });
}
```

## Void pipelines

A command with no result still has a result slot: it carries `Unit`. That is what lets a
post-interceptor written against a void command receive something well-defined:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCompletion : ICommandPostInterceptor<ArchiveOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        ArchiveOrder command, object result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Archived {Id}", command.Id);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

`Unit` is a value, not `null`. Return what you were handed.

## Ordering

`[Weight]` descending, direct before covariant — the same rule as every stage. When results are
being transformed, order matters more than usual, because each interceptor sees what the last
one returned:

```csharp
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Redact  : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetCustomer, CustomerDto> { … }
[Weight(50)]  public sealed class Envelope: IQueryPostInterceptor<GetCustomer, CustomerDto> { … }
```

## Typical uses

**Caching**, populating on the way out while a pre-interceptor checks on the way in.

**Enveloping** a result into a transport shape, when you would otherwise write it in every
handler.

**Metrics and audit** for successful operations specifically — the failure side belongs to the
exception or final stage.

**Appending to an outbox** — though be careful about ordering relative to a transaction commit;
see the [Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## What does not belong here

**Cleanup.** A post-interceptor does not run when the handler threw. Anything that must happen
either way goes in a final interceptor.

**Failure translation.** That is the exception stage, which can answer the dispatch with a
result.

**Work that should be part of the result.** If the handler's result is incomplete without this
interceptor, the handler is incomplete. An interceptor that every caller depends on is not
cross-cutting — it is the operation.
